  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Rain continues to move southeast this afternoon, \nwith gradual subsidence setting in behind the passing mid-lvl \nvorticity. There remain a very low risks for a few elevated \nstorms to form along the MO river this afternoon, and then \nfurther northwest into central South Dakota where a narrow \ntongue of increased boundary layer moisture remains. \n\nTONIGHT: Much of the evening and overnight hours is expected to \nremain quiet and dry. While a few showers and thunderstorms may\nform over central and eastern ND and perhaps extreme northern \nSouth Dakota. Winds at the surface will turn light and variable,\nand with recent rain and mostly clear skies some areas of fog \nmay develop by daybreak. The lowest visibilities may form along \nthe I-29 corridor with locally dense fog possible at times. \n\nTHURSDAY: Another embedded wave within the northwesterly flow \npattern is expected to drop through the Plains and into the Upper \nMidwest on Thursday. This wave will push a surface front into the \nTri-State are in the afternoon with breezy southwesterly winds ahead \nof the front in the morning.  Soundings ahead of the boundary by \nafternoon show moderate instability upwards of 2500 J/KG in a \nfew locations. However they also show at least 100 J/KG CIN, \nwhich could serve as a big limiting factor for convective \ndevelopment in the afternoon. The greatest forcing along the \nboundary remains focused from northern Minnesota into central \nMinnesota, with much weaker surface convergence along the \nboundary further southwest into northern Nebraska. This is \nlikely why, outside of any significant moisture/thermal \nadvection in the boundary layer, CAMs remain very isolated in \nconvective development by late afternoon and early evening. That\nsaid, given effective shear of 35-40 knots, any updraft could \nrotate and increase the hail size towards the 1.5-2\" mark. \n\nFRIDAY: We may see a quick warmup on Friday into the middle and \nupper 80s as brief mid-lvl ridging moves into the Tri-state area. By \nthe afternoon however, model guidance is all over the place \nsuggesting perhaps another weak trough passes through the \nPlains/Midwest late in the day. Should this wave materialize, it \ncould drive a front and some associated convection through the \neastern Dakotas Friday afternoon and evening. This remains a very \nlow-probability event, so will keep PoPs on the lower end of \nguidance. \n\nSATURDAY-SUNDAY: Much of the upcoming weekend will be dry as \nCanadian high pressure moves through the region. Light northerly \nwinds and weak CAA aloft will promote a very pleasant afternoon with \nnortherly surface winds and lower humidity. Some modest return \nflow and weak warm advection may develop over the western \nDakotas and Nebraska panhandle into Sunday. Medium range \nguidance has slowed down this eastward progression over the past\n2 days, now indicating that precipitation chances should remain\nwest of the CWA until Sunday night into Monday. High \ntemperatures Sunday will be very similar to Saturday, in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s. \n\nMONDAY-TUESDAY: Precipitation chances increase early next week as a \nshortwave crests the top of the growing mid-lvl ridge over the \ncentral CONUS.  Model spread in both timing and location of \nprecipitation will keep these PoPs only around the 20-30% \nrange. Temperatures do show a warming trend into next week, with\ngrowing probabilities of highs reaching or exceeding the 85 \nmark at times into mid-week.",
